    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by the explosive speed-power combination of **Trea Turner** (75.42) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (71.15). **Trea Turner** is our premier play, benefiting from a soft matchup against Freeland\u2019s 4.24 xERA and a significant +10% boost from his leadoff spot. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** remains a priority start despite his #8 projection; his 94% stolen base success rate against the slow delivery of Leiter makes him a category-winner for our specific 5x5 needs. **Kyle Schwarber** (71.37) rounds out the top tier, utilizing his elite .396 xwOBA to punish Freeland, making this top-of-the-order stack our most reliable path to R and HR accumulation.\n\nSeveral secondary plays offer high upside through favorable situational geometry. **Colt Keith** (67.35) and **Junior Caminero** (66.72) are our most efficient mid-tier pivots, both capitalizing on +14% to +15% combined park and SP skill advantages. **Kazuma Okamoto** (64.93) is our strongest UTIL play, as he faces the league\u2019s most favorable SP matchup today against Kochanowicz (5.14 xERA). While **Yordan Alvarez** (61.66) faces an elite arm in Burns (2.91 xERA), his raw talent profile keeps him as an auto-start; conversely, **Caleb Durbin** remains vital due to the wind-out conditions at his park, effectively buffering his otherwise lower order position. \n\nThe decision-making process for the final utility spots was tight, with **Chase Meidroth** (51.75) narrowly squeezing into the MI slot over bench alternatives. The optimizer favored **Chase Meidroth** due to the beneficial wind conditions, which slightly edged out the baseline expectations for our reserve pool. We are monitoring **Gary Sanchez** and **Dillon Dingler** closely; both are listed as \"Lineup Pending,\" and should their official batting positions drop below the #4/5 hole, their efficiency scores will crater. With **Christian Yelich**, **Zac Veen**, **Lars Nootbaar**, and **Luke Raley** currently 